{{smaller block|Those you were wont to take delight in, the ''tragedians'' of the city.{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, ii. 2. 342.}}}}

'''tragedienne''' (trạ̄-jē′di-en; F. pron. tra-zhā-dien′), ''n.'' [< F. ''tragédienne'', fem. of ''tragédien'', tragedian: see ''tragedian.''] A female actor of tragedy; a tragic actress.

'''tragedious†''' (trạ̄-jē′di-us), ''a.'' [< ME. ''tragedyous'', < OF. *''tragedios'' (= Sp. ''tragedioso''), < ''tragedie'', tragedy: see ''tragedy.''] Tragic; tragical.

{{smaller block|Of whom tedyous it is to me to wryte the ''tragedyous'' hystory, except that I remembre that good it is to wryte and put in remembraunce the punysshment of synners.{{right|''Fabyan'', Chron.}}}}

'''tragedy''' (traj′e-di), ''n.''; pl. ''tragedies'' (-diz). [< ME. ''tragedie, tragedye'', < OF. ''tragedie'', F. ''tragédie'' = Sp. Pg. It. ''tragedia'', < L. ''tragœdia'', ML. also ''tragedia'', tragedy, a tragedy, lofty style, a great commotion or disturbance, < Gr. τραγῳδία, a tragedy (see def.), serious poetry, an exaggerated speech, a melancholy event, < τραγῳδός (> L. ''tragœdus''), a tragic actor or singer, lit. ‘a goat-singer,’ < τράγος, a goat, he-goat (lit. ‘nibbler,’ < τρώγειν, τραγεῖν, nibble), + ᾠδός, contr. of ἀοιδός, a singer (cf. ᾠδή, ἀοιδή, a song), < ἀείδειν, ᾄδειν, sing (see ''ode''<sup>1</sup>), and same termination appears in ''comedy''. The orig. reason of the name τραγῳδός, ‘goat-singer,’ is uncertain. (''a'') In one view, so called because a goat was the prize for the best performance. This would require τραγῳδός to mean ‘singer for a goat,’ and would make the name for a distinctive character or act depend on a subsequent fact, namely, the goat given at the end of the performance to only one of the performers. (''b'') In another

readings.c3s1:
¶
{{smaller block|This man's brow, like to a title-leaf,
Foretells the nature of a ''tragic'' volume.
{{right|''Shak.'', 2. Hen. IV., i. 1. 60.}}}}

2. Characteristic of tragedy.

{{smaller block|And so it is that we discover the true majesty of human nature itself, in the ''tragic'' grandeur of its disorders, nowhere else. {{right|''Bushnell'', Sermons for New Life, p. 64.}}}}

3. Connected with or characterized by great calamity, cruelty, or bloodshed; mournful; dreadful; heart-rending.

{{smaller block|Woe than Byron's woe more ''tragic'' far.
{{right|''M. Arnold'', A Picture at Newstead.}}
All things grew more ''tragic'' and more strange.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Princess, vi.}}}}

4. Expressive of tragedy, death, or sorrow.

{{smaller block|I now must change
Those notes to ''tragic.'' {{right|''Milton'', P. L., ix. 6.}}}}

II. ''n.'' 1. A writer of tragedy; a tragedian.

{{smaller block|The Comicks are called διδασκαλοι, of the Greeks, no less than the ''tragicks.'' {{right|''B. Jonson'', Discoveries.}}}}

2. A tragedy; a tragic drama. ''Prior.'' (''Imp. Dict.'')

'''tragical''' (traj′i-kạl), ''a.'' [< ''tragic'' + ''-al.''] Same as ''tragic.''

{{smaller block|Hoping the consequence
Will prove as bitter, black, and ''tragical.''
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. III., iv. 4. 7.}}}}

'''tragically''' (traj′i-kạl-i), ''adv.'' 1. In a tragic manner; in a manner befitting tragedy.

{{smaller block|His [Juvenal's] own genius . . . was sharp and eager; . . . and as his provocations were great, he has revenged them ''tragically.'' {{right|''Dryden'', Essay on Satire.}}}}

2. Mournfully; sorrowfully.

{{smaller block|Many complain and cry out very ''tragically'' of the wretchedness of their hearts. {{right|''South'', Sermons, VI. xii.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
'''tragicalness''' (traj′i-ka̤l-nes), ''n.'' Tragic character or quality; mournfulness; sadness; fatality.

{{smaller block|We moralize the fable . . . in the ''tragicalness'' of the event.{{right|''Decay of Christ. Piety.''}}}}

'''tragici''', ''n.'' Plural of ''tragicus.''

'''tragicly†''' (traj′ik-li), ''adv.'' [< ''tragic'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] Tragically; sadly; mournfully.

{{smaller block|I shall sadly sing, too ''tragickly'' inclin'd.{{right|''Stirling'', Aurora, Elegy, iii.}}}}

'''tragicomedy''' (traj-i-kom′e-di), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. ''tragycomedie''; < F. ''tragicomédie'' = Sp. Pg. ''tragicomedia'' = It. ''tragicomedia'', < ML. *''tragicomœdia'', a contraction of L. ''tragicocomœdia'', < Gr. *τραγικοκωμῳδία, < τραγικός, tragic, + κωμῳδία, comedy: see ''tragic'' and ''comedy''.] A dramatic composition in which serious and comic scenes are blended; a composition partaking of the nature of both tragedy and comedy, and of which the event is not unhappy, as Shakspere's "Measure for Measure."

{{smaller block|Neither the admiration and commiseration, nor the right sportfulnes, is by their mungrell ''Tragy-comedie'' obtained.{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Apol. for Poetrie.}}}}

{{smaller block|Such acts and scenes hath this ''tragi-comedy'' of love.{{right|''Burton'', Anat. of Mel., p. 525.}}}}

'''tragicomic''' (traj-i-kom′ik), ''a.'' [< F. ''tragicomique'' = Sp. ''tragicómico'' = Pg. It. ''tragicomico'', < L. as if *''tragicomicus'', contr. of *''tragicocomicus''; as ''tragic'' + ''comic''. Cf. ''tragicomedy''.] Pertaining to tragicomedy; characterized by both serious and comic scenes.

readings.c3s3:
¶
{{smaller block|In viewing this monstrous ''tragicomic'' scene, the most opposite passions necessarily succeed.
{{right|''Burke'', Rev. in France.}}}}

{{smaller block|Julian felt towards him that ''tragi-comic'' sensation which makes us pity the object which excites it, not the less that we are somewhat inclined to laugh amid our sympathy.
{{right|''Scott'', Peveril of the Peak, xxxvi.}}}}

{{smaller block|They [Shelley and his wife] wandered vaguely about after this, in Scotland one time, in Wales the next, meeting with all kinds of ''tragi-comic'' adventures.
{{right|''Mrs. Oliphant'', Lit. Hist. Eng., III. 39.}}}}

'''tragicomical''' (traj-i-kom′i-kạl), ''a.'' [< ''tragicomic'' + ''-al.''] Same as ''tragicomic. Sir P. Sidney'', Apol. for Poetry.

'''tragicomically''' (traj-i-kom′i-kạl-i), ''adv.'' In a tragicomic manner.

'''tragicomipastoral''' (traj-i-kom-i-pȧs′tọr-ạl), ''a.'' [Irreg. < ''tragicomi(c)'' + ''pastoral.''] Partaking of the nature of tragedy, comedy, and pastoral poetry. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|The whole art of ''tragicomipastoral'' farce lies in interweaving of the several kinds of the drama with each other, so that they can not be distinguished or separated.
{{right|''Gay'', What d'ye Call it (ed. 1715), Pref.}}}}

'''tragicus''' (traj′i-kus), ''n.''; pl. ''tragici'' (-sī). [NL. (sc. ''musculus'', muscle), < ''tragus'', q. v.] A muscle of the pinna of the ear which actuates the tragus. {{smaller|In man it is rudimentary, practically functionless, and confined to the part named; but its character in other mammals varies and may be very different.}}

